WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.280
Why did "Walking Tall" fall flat? Why did "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:08.000
Rundown" get the runaround? And how did&nbsp;
Dwayne Johnson save not just his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.480
but the entire "Fast &amp; Furious"&nbsp;
franchise? Keep watching to find out!

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.880
You couldn't ask for a more high-profile movie&nbsp;
star debut than when Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:23.520
snagged the villain role in mega-sequel&nbsp;
"The Mummy Returns." Despite only being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.080
in a a small part of the movie, Johnson was a&nbsp;
huge part of "The Mummy Returns'" marketing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.200
leading to a $68 million&nbsp;
opening and a $435 global haul.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:39.360
Johnson snagged $5.5 million for the prequel "The&nbsp;
Scorpion King," beer and pizza money to him today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.520
but at the time the highest-salary&nbsp;
ever for a first-time leading man.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:46.560
Johnson proved he was worth the&nbsp;
investment, with the movie bringing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:52.640
in $165 million worldwide. Then,&nbsp;
"The Rundown" happened. Whoops!

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:58.000
Honestly, "The Rundown" is a really solid&nbsp;
action movie, with a 69% critics score on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.320
Rotten Tomatoes. However, by 2003 "one man army"&nbsp;
action movies were being replaced by superheroes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:10.240
The movie managed to pull in $80 million&nbsp;
worldwide, but with an $85 million price tag, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.280
underperformance of "The Rundown" nearly sent The&nbsp;
Rock scrambling right back to the wrestling ring.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.240
"Fast &amp; Furious Presents: Hobbs &amp;&nbsp;
Shaw" may have caused a rift with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.680
"the family," but it did pretty&nbsp;
well for Johnson's bank account,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.240
and for Universal Pictures which bankrolled&nbsp;
it. This spin-off from "The Fast &amp; Furious"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.280
franchise was one of the few bright spots&nbsp;
of the dismal summer box office of 2019.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.600
While it certainly helps being part of a&nbsp;
"Fast &amp; Furious" franchise that has racked up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:43.440
$6.5 billion worldwide, Johnson's global&nbsp;
popularity played a huge part in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.640
success of "Hobbs &amp; Shaw." All due respect to&nbsp;
Tyrese Gibson and Chris "Ludacris" Bridges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.720
but it seems unlikely a "Pearch &amp;&nbsp;
Parker" film would do nearly as well.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:57.520
So, how well did "Hobbs &amp; Shaw" do?&nbsp;
With a mammoth $200 million budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.800
$20 million of which reportedly&nbsp;
went to Johnson's wallet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:07.840
"Hobbs &amp; Shaw" made $173 million&nbsp;
domestically and $760 million worldwide.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:13.680
2005 was a time of transition for Johnson.&nbsp;
The lukewarm box office response to action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.240
pics like "The Rundown" stalled his efforts&nbsp;
at becoming the next Arnold Schwarzenegger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:23.280
However, he hadn't yet launched his dreadful&nbsp;
"macho guy in a kid's movie" phase exemplified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:28.720
by films like "Race To Witch Mountain."
In this muck was the appropriately titled "Doom."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:33.600
Appropriately titled because that's exactly&nbsp;
what this movie met critically and commercially:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.600
Doom. With an 18% critics score and 34%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:43.120
audience score, the video game adaptation failed&nbsp;
to transition from the console to the big screen.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:50.320
With a $70 million budget, "Doom" finished its run&nbsp;
earning $28 million domestically and $58 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:56.160
worldwide. Add in marketing costs, and it's&nbsp;
estimated the film likely lost around $82 million.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:02.240
Johnson was only a co-lead opposite Karl Urban,&nbsp;
but his name and face were plastered all over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:07.200
the promotional materials, so whether he was&nbsp;
in it for a few minutes or the whole runtime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:12.160
people who went to see it did so because&nbsp;
of him - so the failure was considered his.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:13.463
"I'm not supposed to die!"

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.160
Dwayne Johnson's second appearance as Special&nbsp;
Agent Luke Hobbs is one of the biggest hits on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.120
his resume. This was "Fast and Furious 6," by&nbsp;
the way, and if you have trouble remembering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:30.240
which "Furious" movie that was, it was the one&nbsp;
where Hobbs hires Dominic and the family to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:35.280
out a mercenary - whose second in command&nbsp;
is Letty - in exchange for a full pardon.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:40.000
Not the the plot matters. "Fast and&nbsp;
Furious 6" earned a whopping $238&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:46.720
million domestically and $789 million&nbsp;
worldwide. On a $160 million budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:51.040
it is estimated to have made&nbsp;
a profit around $469 million.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:57.040
You could be forgiven for thinking: "So what?&nbsp;
Johnson just latched onto a popular franchise."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:02.480
Actually, it was kinda the other way around.&nbsp;
Johnson's first "Furious" movie, "Fast Five,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:09.760
was already a huge 44% improvement over the&nbsp;
"Fast &amp; Furious'" take of $359 million. Johnson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:15.920
helped the "Furious" franchise level up in a big&nbsp;
way, leading to his nickname "Franchise Viagra."

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:21.840
Dwayne Johnson may have starred in "Walking&nbsp;
Tall," but he certainly wasn't walking very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:27.120
tall at the box office. "The Rundown" had&nbsp;
disappointed with $80 million worldwide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.200
so Johnson was looking for a rebound.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:34.400
And he did not get it. "Walking Tall" wasn't&nbsp;
a course correction, so much as an even bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:40.960
detour than "The Rundown." "Walking Tall" got a&nbsp;
dreadful 26% critics' score and a barely fresh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:47.600
60% audience score. Its box office was even&nbsp;
worse, earning just $47 million worldwide.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:53.040
Johnson's series of bombs showed while audiences&nbsp;
were all in on him being an "added value" to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:58.320
the already popular "Mummy" series, they&nbsp;
didn't see him as a movie star ... yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:03.040
He was still "the wrestler who acted," as&nbsp;
evidenced by his continued billing as The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:08.080
Rock. But if he was going to "walk tall"&nbsp;
for real, a change was clearly in order.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:09.840
"That was a hell of a homecoming party."

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:15.440
The "Fast and Furious" and "Jumanji"&nbsp;
franchises are similarly unusual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:20.000
because they started as successful, but&nbsp;
not spectacular, box office performers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:25.920
only to become major franchises later. So&nbsp;
what's the common denominator? Dwayne Johnson.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:29.760
Johnson's impact on "Jumanji" might&nbsp;
be more impressive than "Fast and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.440
Furious," as "Jumanji" wasn't&nbsp;
even a franchise before Johnson;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:38.000
it was a well-remembered Robin Williams&nbsp;
star vehicle from 22 years earlier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:43.360
Johnson turned it into a franchise with&nbsp;
2017's "Jumanji: Welcome to the Jungle."

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:47.760
Sony wisely played it safe, only&nbsp;
spending $90 million on the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:51.600
and making sure "Jumanji" was front&nbsp;
and center in the film's marketing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:56.960
That, and plastering the stars' mugs all over&nbsp;
the marketing, most notably that of Johnson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:02.080
It turned out to be a good strategy. "Jumanji:&nbsp;
Welcome To The Jungle" opened relatively small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:08.480
to just $36 million, but it legged it out&nbsp;
to an incredible $404 million domestically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:17.200
and $961 million worldwide, for an estimated&nbsp;
$781 million profit. Yup, somehow, someway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:23.120
the "Jumanji" series went from a has-been to&nbsp;
a nearly $1 billion franchise. This makes it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:27.760
not only one of Johnson's biggest hits, but&nbsp;
the biggest franchise transformation ever.

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:35.120
By 2006, Dwayne Johnson's movie star&nbsp;
career was off to a "rocky" start.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.360
Actually, it was straight-up terrible.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:41.200
"The Rundown," "Walking Tall,"&nbsp;
and "Doom," had each bombed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:46.400
demonstrating moviegoers weren't looking for the&nbsp;
"next" Schwarzenegger, and even if they were,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:51.760
it wasn't Johnson. So what's a would-be&nbsp;
macho, one-man-army movie star supposed to do?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:58.720
Give the audience the feels. Enter 2006's&nbsp;
"Gridiron Gang," a "based on a true story" drama&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:02.800
in which Johnson plays a probation&nbsp;
officer and counselor at a juvenile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:07.680
detention center who teaches the inmates&nbsp;
responsibility by forming a football team.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.040
If it feels like you've seen this&nbsp;
movie before, critics agreed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:17.280
panning it as cliche with a 42% score. And&nbsp;
audiences stayed away. "Gridiron Gang" earned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:23.920
$41 million worldwide. Even with a modest $30&nbsp;
million budget, it lost about $19 million.

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:28.400
The only silver lining is that&nbsp;
it allowed Johnson to demonstrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:32.000
actual acting ability, which might&nbsp;
have helped him get another chance.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:38.720
The original 1995 "Jumanji" was a solid&nbsp;
hit for Robin Williams. But when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:44.240
studio tried to turn it into a franchise&nbsp;
a decade later with 2005's "Zathura,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:49.040
they made the mistake of not bringing back&nbsp;
Williams. As a result, the movie was a flop.

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:55.040
So after the success of 2017's "Jumanji: Welcome&nbsp;
To The Jungle, the studio had learned its lesson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:58.480
bringing back Johnson and his&nbsp;
co-stars to recapture the magic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:03.120
It worked: 2019's "Jumanji: The&nbsp;
Next Level" was another smash hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:08.320
earning $316 million domestically&nbsp;
and $800 million worldwide.

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:10.400
"Awright! Bring it on!"

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:16.240
Thanks to its modest $125 million&nbsp;
budget, it's estimated that "Jumanji:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:21.760
The Next Level" earned around $550&nbsp;
million. That's some serious bank.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:26.560
Dwayne Johnson's movie career&nbsp;
was in a weird place in 2010.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:31.600
He'd headlined several action movie bombs,&nbsp;
but found his new niche starring in "badass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:36.880
in a kid's movie" films like "The Game Plan"&nbsp;
and "Tooth Fairy." Alas, those brought to mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:41.600
Hulk Hogan's failed big screen career,&nbsp;
such as the dreadful "Mr. Nanny". Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:46.560
they made a little money, but is that really the&nbsp;
direction anyone would want to take their career?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:52.000
Certainly not Johnson, who tried again to&nbsp;
headline an action flick in 2010's "Faster."

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:56.960
With a very modest $24 million budget,&nbsp;
"Faster" seemed sure to make money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:02.000
if only because it was so cheap to&nbsp;
produce. Yeah, so much for that.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:08.080
"Faster" tanked out of the gate and ran out of&nbsp;
gas fast, earning $23 million domestically and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:15.280
$35 million worldwide. Despite its bargain budget,&nbsp;
"Faster" still reportedly lost about $13 million.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:22.240
Back to kiddie movie hell for Johnson? Not quite.&nbsp;
He followed this up with 2011's "Fast Five,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:27.920
which more than doubled the worldwide gross of&nbsp;
"Faster" in its opening weekend alone. Johnson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:32.920
action movie career didn't need to go "Faster;"&nbsp;
it just needed to go Faster and Furious-er.

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:39.920
Few franchises have followed a more interesting&nbsp;
trajectory than "The Fast and The Furious."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:46.320
The series started strong in 2001, hit the skids,&nbsp;
then roared back not only bigger than before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.400
but much bigger. And much of that can be&nbsp;
chalked up to the addition of Johnson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:54.640
who changed the franchise&nbsp;
forever with 2011's "Fast Five."

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:56.960
But that wasn't the biggest hit in the franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:01.520
The series' high water mark&nbsp;
came with 2015's "Furious 7."

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:07.440
You could tell this film was meant for big things&nbsp;
when it opened to $147 million domestically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:14.480
a $50 million bump from the $97 million opening&nbsp;
of "Fast and Furious 6." "Furious 7" wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:21.360
done smashing franchise records, earning $353&nbsp;
million domestically on its way to an incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:30.640
$1.5 billion worldwide, nearly doubling the $789&nbsp;
million worldwide gross of "Fast and Furious 6."

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:36.640
"Furious 7" became one of the top ten&nbsp;
highest-grossing films ever. With a $190 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:45.440
budget, "Furious 7" earned Universal a profit of&nbsp;
around $1.12 billion. "Furious 7" is unequivocally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:52.480
Johnson's biggest hit, handily topping the $781&nbsp;
million profit of "Jumanji: Welcome To The Jungle"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:59.840
and $1.2 billion gross of "Fate of the Furious."&nbsp;
Not bad for a pro wrestler turned actor, right?

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:05.840
There are bombs, there are mega bombs, and then&nbsp;
there's "Southland Tales." It's appropriate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:11.520
film is about a potential nuclear annihilation, as&nbsp;
that pretty much was the scale of this disaster.

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:15.920
Strangely, "Southland Tales" seemed&nbsp;
like it might be just what Johnson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:22.160
career needed in 2007 following a string&nbsp;
of failures. Most of Johnson's mid-2000s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:27.440
oeuvre was straight-to-DVD quality, so&nbsp;
starring in critical darling Richard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:33.360
Kelly's highly-anticipated follow-up to "Donnie&nbsp;
Darko" was probably meant to give him indie cred.

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:35.200
So much for that.

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:39.920
While "Southland Tales" has recently&nbsp;
received something of a critical reappraisal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:46.880
at the time few viewers liked it, as its 39%&nbsp;
critics score and 41% audience score demonstrated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:54.160
Even worse was its box office gross. Ready&nbsp;
for this? "Southland Tales" made $364,607&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.600 --> 00:12:01.360
worldwide. Yep, probably less than what a Luke&nbsp;
Hobbs' movie makes on a Tuesday afternoon.

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:05.840
For "The Fast and The Furious"&nbsp;
franchise's eighth installment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:10.320
the series did what nobody could have&nbsp;
possibly predicted — it broke up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:15.680
family! And Vin Diesel's Dominic "Dom"&nbsp;
Toretto was the one to do it! Say what?!

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:21.120
Honestly, by this point the franchise could have&nbsp;
done just about anything and still would have made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:25.520
boatloads of money. But just for the&nbsp;
sake of thoroughness, here's the deets:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:31.040
after Dom fell into the seductive clutches of&nbsp;
Charlize Theron's diabolical Cipher, there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:36.400
only one man who could bring the family together&nbsp;
to take on their former leader — Dwayne Johnson.

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:41.680
By this point Johnson was almost as much a part&nbsp;
of this franchise as Diesel, as despite the large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:46.800
ensemble they were the only two featured on the&nbsp;
teaser poster, with the provocative "Family No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:53.120
More" tagline. That was enough. "The Fate of&nbsp;
the Furious" opened to $98 million, and wound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.120 --> 00:13:01.360
up bringing home $225 million domestically and&nbsp;
$1.2 billion worldwide. With a blockbuster-sized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:08.400
$250 million budget, "The Fate of the Furious"&nbsp;
still hauled in about $700 million in profit.

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:13.840
At this point it seems fair to say that Johnson&nbsp;
has probably left the bombs behind for good.

